   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "The sharpest edges, stated as edges",
   "originalText": "The sharpest edges, stated as edges",
   "fontSize": 17,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "hn",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 2326,
   "width": 1011,
   "height": 260,
   "strokeColor": "#64748b",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100140,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700980,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "receiver-side filtering wastes most of the work   a push is delivered to a friend's server and THEN discarded if they are\n                                                  beyond the radius. For users with scattered friends, the overwhelming\n                                                  majority of 13.3M pushes/sec are dropped after delivery. Geohash-prefixed\n                                                  channels - publish to channel:{uid}:{geohash5}, subscribe only to nearby\n                                                  prefixes - would filter BEFORE the push. The clear next optimization.\nprivacy is enforced only at the subscription boundary   a server subscribes to a friend's channel and then filters by\n                                                  distance, so it RECEIVES that friend's exact position even when they are\n                                                  500 miles away and should be invisible. Nothing is shown to the user, but\n                                                  the data crossed a boundary it did not need to. Geohash channels would fix\n                                                  this as a side effect - a good argument for doing them on privacy grounds.\nthe whale problem is capped, not solved           5,000 friends means 500 pushes per update instead of 40, so that channel is\n                                                  12x hotter. Consistent hashing spreads whale channels across nodes; a\n                                                  friend-count limit is a product constraint standing in for an engineering\n                                                  one, which is an uncomfortable place to leave it.\na full-fleet reconnect is a multi-minute recovery  4 billion subscriptions. Staggered reconnect with jitter, sticky routing\n                                                  and batched subscribes reduce it and do not eliminate it.",
   "originalText": "receiver-side filtering wastes most of the work   a push is delivered to a friend's server and THEN discarded if they are\n                                                  beyond the radius. For users with scattered friends, the overwhelming\n                                                  majority of 13.3M pushes/sec are dropped after delivery. Geohash-prefixed\n                                                  channels - publish to channel:{uid}:{geohash5}, subscribe only to nearby\n                                                  prefixes - would filter BEFORE the push. The clear next optimization.\nprivacy is enforced only at the subscription boundary   a server subscribes to a friend's channel and then filters by\n                                                  distance, so it RECEIVES that friend's exact position even when they are\n                                                  500 miles away and should be invisible. Nothing is shown to the user, but\n                                                  the data crossed a boundary it did not need to. Geohash channels would fix\n                                                  this as a side effect - a good argument for doing them on privacy grounds.\nthe whale problem is capped, not solved           5,000 friends means 500 pushes per update instead of 40, so that channel is\n                                                  12x hotter. Consistent hashing spreads whale channels across nodes; a\n                                                  friend-count limit is a product constraint standing in for an engineering\n                                                  one, which is an uncomfortable place to leave it.\na full-fleet reconnect is a multi-minute recovery  4 billion subscriptions. Staggered reconnect with jitter, sticky routing\n                                                  and batched subscribes reduce it and do not eliminate it.",
   "fontSize": 13,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  }
